## Why This Is Asked
Races in io_uring multi-producer submission and memory ordering are subtle in real systems; candidates demonstrate understanding of ring buffers, memory barriers, and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- io_uring submission and completion rings (SQ/CQ)
- Multi-producer correctness in shared ring buffers
- Memory barriers (write/read, acquire/release semantics)
- In-order completion guarantees and request tagging

## Code Example
```c
// Example: multi-producer submission with atomic SQE reservation
struct io_uring ring;
pthread_mutex_t sqe_mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Atomic SQE reservation with sequence numbers
static atomic_uint_fast64_t seq_counter = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);

int submit_io_safe(int fd, void *buf, size_t len, uint64_t *seq_out) {
    // Reserve SQE atomically
    pthread_mutex_lock(&sqe_mutex);
    struct io_uring_sqe *sqe = io_uring_get_sqe(&ring);
    if (!sqe) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&sqe_mutex);
        return -EBUSY;
    }

    // Assign sequence number for ordering
    uint64_t seq = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&seq_counter, 1, 
                                            memory_order_relaxed);
    *seq_out = seq;

    // Prepare submission with sequence tag
    io_uring_prep_write(sqe, fd, buf, len, 0);
    sqe->user_data = seq;

    // Release barrier before submit
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
    int ret = io_uring_submit(&ring);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sqe_mutex);

    return ret;
}

// Completion processing with ordering
void process_completions(void) {
    struct io_uring_cqe *cqe;

    while (io_uring_peek_cqe(&ring, &cqe) == 0) {
        // Acquire barrier before reading completion
        atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_acquire);

        uint64_t seq = cqe->user_data;
        // Process completion in sequence order...

        io_uring_cqe_seen(&ring, cqe);
    }
}
```

## Implementation Strategy
For high-throughput ordered logging, implement per-thread submission queues with a single consumer thread that preserves order while allowing parallel I/O preparation.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of disk scheduling algorithms, end-to-end flow from I/O request to service, and practical data structure choices in kernel-like environments.

## Key Concepts
- **Elevator (SCAN) vs SSTF**: SCAN provides more uniform latency by serving all requests in sweep order, while SSTF can cause starvation for distant requests.
- **Direction management**: Track current direction and handle boundary reversal at min/max sector addresses.
- **Tie-breaking**: When multiple requests target the same sector, FIFO ordering by arrival time ensures fairness.
- **Fairness considerations**: SCAN inherently favors requests at disk edges but avoids starvation. For stronger fairness guarantees, C-SCAN (circular SCAN) provides equal service time per full sweep, or deadline-based schedulers can prioritize older requests.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Req {
    int sector;
    int arrival;
    int pid;
    struct Req* next;
} Req;

Req* enqueue(Req* head, Req* r, int dir) {
    // Insert sorted by sector; if equal, preserve arrival order
    if (!head || r->sector < head->sector || 
        (r->sector == head->sector && r->arrival < head->arrival)) {
        r->next = head;
        return r;
    }
    Req* cur = head;
    while (cur->next && (cur->next->sector < r->sector ||
        (cur->next->sector == r->sector && cur->next->arrival <= r->arrival)))
        cur = cur->next;
    r->next = cur->next;
    cur->next = r;
    return head;
}

Req* next_request(Req* head, int* dir, int minSector, int maxSector) {
    if (!head) return NULL;
    // Seek in current direction
    Req* best = NULL;
    Req** prev = &head;
    Req* cur = head;
    while (cur) {
        if ((*dir > 0 && cur->sector >= 0) || (*dir < 0 && cur->sector <= 0)) {
            if (!best) { best = cur; *prev = cur->next; break; }
        }
        prev = &cur->next;
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    // If no request in current direction, reverse and try again
    if (!best) { *dir = -*dir; return next_request(head, dir, minSector, maxSector); }
    return best;
}
```

